WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:05.634
FedVG: gradient-guided aggregation for enhanced federated
learning.

00:00:06.084 --> 00:00:13.476
From West Virginia University and the University of Aberdeen,
in the CVPR 2026 Findings track.

00:00:13.926 --> 00:00:21.510
The whole paper is about one line of the federated learning
algorithm — the line where the server decides how much each

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:30.612
In federated learning, each client trains locally and sends
its update to a server, which combines them into a new global

00:00:31.062 --> 00:00:37.806
FedAvg does that by weighting each client's update by n-k over
N — its share of the total training data.

00:00:38.256 --> 00:00:46.176
The reasoning is straightforward. A client with more data
probably trained a more reliable model, so it should count for

00:00:46.626 --> 00:00:52.338
That holds when clients look alike. The premise of this paper
is that they do not.

00:00:53.688 --> 00:01:03.048
Under non-IID data, clients differ in class distribution,
image characteristics and how much their local optimum

00:01:03.498 --> 00:01:12.186
A client can hold a large but narrow dataset — many images of
a handful of classes — and train a model that is confident and

00:01:12.636 --> 00:01:17.292
A smaller client with a broader mix might learn far more
useful features.

00:01:17.742 --> 00:01:24.006
Weighting by volume gives the first client more influence than
the second, which is exactly backwards.

00:01:24.456 --> 00:01:30.936
The authors put it bluntly: naive reliance on data volume
overlooks the deeper behaviour of training.

00:01:32.286 --> 00:01:37.758
Here is the problem geometrically, and this is the paper's own
framing in Figure 2.

00:01:38.208 --> 00:01:41.664
Every client starts from the same broadcast model.

00:01:42.114 --> 00:01:46.554
The first descends toward whichever minimum its own data
favours.

00:01:47.004 --> 00:01:52.044
So does the second — somewhere quite different, because it
holds different classes.

00:01:52.494 --> 00:01:56.454
And the third. That divergence is client drift.

00:01:56.904 --> 00:02:05.472
The server then averages those endpoints, and when the clients
disagree strongly the average can land in a high-loss region

00:02:05.922 --> 00:02:12.498
So the question becomes: is there a better signal than dataset
size for deciding whose update to trust?

00:02:13.848 --> 00:02:23.208
Now put both aggregation rules on the same surface, with one
extra thing marked: the global optimum, which is the broad

00:02:23.658 --> 00:02:27.066
The clients start together at the broadcast model.

00:02:27.516 --> 00:02:34.260
Clients one and two descend into narrow, sharp minima — good
for their own data, poor everywhere else.

00:02:34.710 --> 00:02:40.110
Client three lands in a broad, flat basin that sits close to
the global optimum.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:49.680
FedAvg averages the three endpoints with equal weight. That is
the red star, pulled away from the global basin by the two

00:02:50.130 --> 00:03:03.186
FedVG weights by validation gradient. Client three's gradient
is the smallest, because a flat region needs the least

00:03:04.536 --> 00:03:09.048
The answer comes from work on flat minima and sharpness-aware
training.

00:03:09.498 --> 00:03:18.786
Two models can sit at the same loss value in very different
places. One in a broad, flat basin; the other in a narrow,

00:03:19.236 --> 00:03:25.716
The flat one generalises better — small parameter changes
barely move the loss, so it is robust.

00:03:26.166 --> 00:03:36.198
And crucially, you can tell them apart without knowing the
curvature directly. Near a flat minimum the gradient is small.

00:03:36.648 --> 00:03:42.168
So the magnitude of the gradient is a usable proxy for how
well a model will generalise.

00:03:42.618 --> 00:03:50.250
The authors also note it serves as a proxy for epistemic
uncertainty — large gradients mean the model is less

00:03:51.600 --> 00:03:57.312
There is an immediate problem with measuring a gradient. Whose
data do you measure it on?

00:03:57.762 --> 00:04:05.826
Each client holds its own private, skewed dataset — and a
gradient taken there is biased by exactly the skew we are

00:04:06.276 --> 00:04:10.380
The same is true of the second client, and every one after it.

00:04:10.830 --> 00:04:18.654
So FedVG puts a shared validation set on the server, fixed
across all rounds and built from readily available public

00:04:19.104 --> 00:04:25.752
Because it belongs to no client, it is a neutral yardstick —
and no private data has to move for it to work.

00:04:27.102 --> 00:04:36.582
The computation is simple. After a client finishes local
training, the server runs its model on the validation set and

00:04:37.032 --> 00:04:47.760
That gives a gradient for every layer. FedVG takes the norm of
each layer's gradient and averages across all L layers,

00:04:48.210 --> 00:04:53.922
Then it inverts: the score is one over G-bar, normalised so
the scores sum to one.

00:04:54.372 --> 00:05:02.892
A small gradient norm means the model already sits somewhere
generalisable and needs little adjustment, so it earns a

00:05:03.342 --> 00:05:09.246
That is the whole method. It replaces n-k over N and changes
nothing else.

00:05:10.596 --> 00:05:15.588
It is worth seeing the computation itself, because it is
smaller than it sounds.

00:05:16.038 --> 00:05:21.390
A batch from the shared validation set goes forward through
the client's network to a loss.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.776
One backward pass sends gradients back through every layer.

00:05:26.226 --> 00:05:37.938
The server reads the norm of each layer's gradient. Notice
they are not alike: the deeper layers carry much larger

00:05:38.388 --> 00:05:44.556
That average is G-bar. Its reciprocal, normalised across
clients, is the weight.

00:05:45.906 --> 00:05:50.946
The choice to average across layers rather than read a single
number is deliberate.

00:05:51.396 --> 00:06:00.492
Prior work shows layers converge at different rates: shallow
layers reach flatter minima with stable gradients, while

00:06:00.942 --> 00:06:09.558
The appendix confirms this in the federated setting — later
layers carry consistently larger and more variable gradient

00:06:10.008 --> 00:06:23.064
There is also a reason not to use validation loss instead. The
classifier head absorbs most of the local bias, so the loss

00:06:23.514 --> 00:06:29.826
Gradients across all layers give a more structural picture of
where a client's representation has drifted.

00:06:31.176 --> 00:06:35.928
The paper also grounds the choice theoretically, which is
worth a moment.

00:06:36.378 --> 00:06:44.730
The gradient of the cross-entropy loss is the negative score
function, and the second moment of the score function is the

00:06:45.180 --> 00:06:52.284
Fisher information measures curvature — how much the model's
output distribution shifts under a small parameter change.

00:06:52.734 --> 00:06:57.966
Large Fisher means a sharp region; small Fisher means a broad,
stable one.

00:06:58.416 --> 00:07:10.008
So the L2 norm of the gradient is, up to a square root, a
diagonal approximation of the Fisher information. Weighting by

00:07:10.458 --> 00:07:15.066
That turns an intuition about flat minima into something with
a footing.

00:07:16.416 --> 00:07:21.072
Now the design consequence, which is the part I find most
useful.

00:07:21.522 --> 00:07:32.346
Almost every federated algorithm fits one template: take the
global model, subtract a weighted sum of client updates, and

00:07:32.796 --> 00:07:42.972
FedAvg sets that regulariser to zero. FedDyn uses a dynamic
term, FedAvgM adds momentum, Scaffold adds control variates.

00:07:43.422 --> 00:07:47.166
All of them use s-k equal to n-k over N.

00:07:47.616 --> 00:07:55.416
FedVG swaps only the s-k. Client-side optimisation, local
epochs, regularisation — none of it changes.

00:07:55.866 --> 00:08:02.370
So it is not a competing algorithm so much as a replaceable
component, and that is how they evaluate it.

00:08:03.720 --> 00:08:08.760
Before the numbers, it is worth seeing what the Dirichlet
parameter does to the data.

00:08:09.210 --> 00:08:19.170
At alpha of 100 every client holds roughly the same mix of
classes. This is close to the IID case that federated

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:25.836
At alpha of 0.05 most clients hold one or two classes almost
exclusively.

00:08:26.286 --> 00:08:34.614
A model trained locally on that will be confident and badly
skewed — and under FedAvg it still gets weighted purely by how

00:08:35.964 --> 00:08:45.372
The experiments sweep heterogeneity using a Dirichlet
parameter alpha: 100 is nearly IID, 0.05 is severely skewed.

00:08:45.822 --> 00:08:54.582
At alpha of 100 everything is within about a point of
everything else. The problem is easy and the aggregation rule

00:08:55.032 --> 00:09:05.400
As alpha falls, the methods separate. At 0.1 FedVG reaches
61.06 against FedAvg's 57.93.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:19.650
At 0.05, the hardest setting, FedVG is at 53.58 and FedAvg at
48.83 — and Scaffold, which was the best method in the easy

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:28.380
That reversal is the pattern worth noticing. Methods that win
when clients are similar are not the ones that hold up when

00:09:29.730 --> 00:09:35.298
The paper's Figure 3 puts all three main datasets side by side
as alpha falls.

00:09:35.748 --> 00:09:41.412
On CIFAR-10, the methods are indistinguishable at the left and
fan apart at the right.

00:09:41.862 --> 00:09:47.214
On OrganAMNIST, FedVG is above every baseline across the whole
range.

00:09:47.664 --> 00:09:52.584
On COVID19 the separation at the far right is the widest of
the three.

00:09:53.034 --> 00:10:00.618
Note the shaded bands as well as the lines. FedVG's are
consistently narrow, which is the stability claim.

00:10:01.968 --> 00:10:07.776
The method is not tied to convolutional networks, so they
repeat it on Vision Transformers.

00:10:08.226 --> 00:10:13.842
ViT-S/16 on COVID19 and ViT-B/16 on DermaMNIST.

00:10:14.292 --> 00:10:20.844
Here FedVG ranks among the top performers rather than clearly
leading, and the paper says exactly that.

00:10:21.294 --> 00:10:30.246
On DermaMNIST it reaches 76.20 at the hardest setting, the
best of any method, but the margins are narrower than on the

00:10:31.596 --> 00:10:37.404
The clearest single result is COVID19 at alpha equals 0.05.

00:10:37.854 --> 00:10:49.734
FedVG reaches 75.18 percent. FedAvg manages 64.92, and the
next best baseline, Scaffold, reaches 70.05.

00:10:50.184 --> 00:10:55.752
That is a margin of over ten points on a medical imaging task
under realistic skew.

00:10:56.202 --> 00:11:04.842
FedVG also has the lowest standard deviation across five seeds
here — 1.36, against five and a half for FedAvg.

00:11:05.292 --> 00:11:13.284
They run Wilcoxon signed-rank tests throughout, and report
that no baseline significantly outperforms FedVG at any alpha.

00:11:14.634 --> 00:11:20.682
Because FedVG only changes the weighting term, they test it
inside all six baselines.

00:11:21.132 --> 00:11:32.100
The gains are largest where the base method struggles. FedAvgM
on OrganAMNIST goes from 60.15 to 77.01 — nearly seventeen

00:11:32.550 --> 00:11:43.734
Elastic is a strong competitor in its own right, and it gains
too: over nine points on COVID19 and eight on OrganAMNIST. So

00:11:44.184 --> 00:11:48.192
FedProx picks up around eight points on COVID19.

00:11:48.642 --> 00:11:56.466
But it does not help everywhere, and they say so. Scaffold
loses two points on CIFAR-10 and FedDyn nearly seven.

00:11:56.916 --> 00:12:06.468
Those two already carry their own drift correction — control
variates and dynamic regularisation — so a second correction

00:12:07.818 --> 00:12:17.586
The obvious objection to the whole approach is the validation
set. If it has to resemble the client data, the method is far

00:12:18.036 --> 00:12:29.028
Start from the reference points: FedAvg with no validation set
reaches 48.83, and FedVG with an in-distribution set reaches

00:12:29.478 --> 00:12:41.214
Now replace that set with genuinely separate public data.
STL-10 shares nine classes with CIFAR-10 and gives 53.85 —

00:12:41.664 --> 00:12:48.216
CIFAR-100, where only three classes map across, still reaches
52.62.

00:12:48.666 --> 00:12:56.946
So the signal does not appear to need a carefully matched
validation set. That is the most practically important result

00:12:58.296 --> 00:13:04.080
A second worry about the validation set: in practice it may
not be class-balanced either.

00:13:04.530 --> 00:13:10.650
They control that with a ratio rho. At rho of one the set
holds every class equally.

00:13:11.100 --> 00:13:15.972
As rho falls the set becomes progressively dominated by a few
classes.

00:13:16.422 --> 00:13:23.070
The method's premise is that this set is a neutral reference,
so the question is how neutral it actually has to be.

00:13:24.420 --> 00:13:30.204
A second worry about the validation set: in practice it may
not be class-balanced either.

00:13:30.654 --> 00:13:39.246
They deliberately imbalance it, controlled by a ratio rho,
where rho of one is perfectly balanced and smaller values are

00:13:39.696 --> 00:13:45.144
FedVG stays above FedAvg across the whole range, even at high
imbalance.

00:13:45.594 --> 00:13:54.546
That matters because the method's whole premise is that the
validation set is a neutral reference. This tests how neutral

00:13:55.896 --> 00:14:01.152
Before the result, the setup, because it is what makes this
ablation convincing.

00:14:01.602 --> 00:14:06.498
Nine clients are heterogeneous — each dominated by one or two
classes.

00:14:06.948 --> 00:14:13.908
A tenth holds a balanced mix of everything. It should learn
the best features, so it should earn the highest weight.

00:14:14.358 --> 00:14:24.990
Crucially, every client holds the same number of images.
Dataset size cannot explain any difference, so whatever the

00:14:26.340 --> 00:14:32.388
This is the most direct test of whether the mechanism does
what it claims, and it is worth the detail.

00:14:32.838 --> 00:14:44.502
They build nine heterogeneous clients with identical dataset
sizes, then add a tenth that is perfectly balanced. That

00:14:44.952 --> 00:14:53.520
With the L1 norm, and with L2, the bold line climbs steadily
above the others over two hundred rounds. The method finds it.

00:14:53.970 --> 00:15:03.570
With the spectral norm, and with a delta norm measuring
distance from the global model, the bold line stays in the

00:15:04.020 --> 00:15:11.268
Because every client holds the same amount of data, this
isolates the mechanism completely from the volume confound.

00:15:12.618 --> 00:15:16.746
A natural extension is to form more than one weight per
client.

00:15:17.196 --> 00:15:23.292
Modelwise is what we have described: average every layer's
gradient norm into a single score.

00:15:23.742 --> 00:15:31.134
Blockwise groups the layers into residual blocks and forms one
score per block, so different depths can be weighted

00:15:31.584 --> 00:15:34.944
Layerwise goes all the way — one score per layer.

00:15:35.394 --> 00:15:41.970
The gradients are identical in all three. What changes is how
many numbers you turn them into.

00:15:43.320 --> 00:15:49.776
A natural extension is a separate weight per layer, or per
residual block, rather than one per client.

00:15:50.226 --> 00:15:56.634
On CIFAR-10 and OrganAMNIST the simplest option wins —
modelwise, one score per client.

00:15:57.084 --> 00:16:03.036
But on COVID19 and DermaMNIST the finer variants are
competitive or slightly ahead.

00:16:03.486 --> 00:16:07.710
And on the ViT models no granularity consistently leads.

00:16:08.160 --> 00:16:17.040
The paper's conclusion is appropriately hedged: the best
granularity depends on architecture, dataset and how

00:16:18.390 --> 00:16:28.926
To summarise: FedVG replaces dataset size with a measure of
generalisation, computed as the average layerwise gradient

00:16:29.376 --> 00:16:35.760
Smaller gradients mean flatter minima, which the Fisher
information connection makes more than an analogy.

00:16:36.210 --> 00:16:42.450
It is modular by construction, so it improves most existing
methods rather than competing with them.

00:16:42.900 --> 00:16:49.308
And the advantage shows up precisely where federated learning
is hardest — highly heterogeneous clients.

00:16:49.758 --> 00:16:52.806
Two caveats the paper raises itself.

00:16:53.256 --> 00:17:03.240
The server needs a public validation set of the right
modality. The external-dataset experiments show it need not

00:17:03.690 --> 00:17:16.818
And the authors flag sensitivity to overlap between that
validation set and particular clients. Shared domains or

00:17:17.268 --> 00:17:25.404
Computing the gradients also costs a backward pass per client
per round — though that falls on the server, not on resource-

